  "worked_solution": "We notice that:\n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $1$ to $\\sqrt{2}$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $0$ to $1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
